the cbs this morning saturday the covet  19 pandemic has put a spotlight on  so-called wet markets where  animals are sold and sometimes  slaughtered scientists believe a wet  market in wuhan china could be where the  coronavirus first jumped from animals to  humans  now there's a growing effort around the  world to shut these markets down  and as jim axelrod shows us hundreds of  them are right here  in the u.s last month small groups of  animal rights protesters gathered at  some of the more than  70 live animal markets that operate  legally around new york city  these are not the same type of markets  like those in china that sell exotic  animals  generally when people are talking about  wet markets they're talking about  live animal markets where  either livestock or wildlife for exotic  animals can be sold  dr ben lotman an infectious disease  epidemiologist at the rollins school of  public health at emory university  says the live animal markets here pose  their own  significant health threat anywhere that  humans  come into contact with animals is a  potential  exposure there's a potential opportunity  for an infection to jump from animals to  humans you know live markets they might  pose a special risk because of the  diversity of animals that are sold  according to the cdc more than six out  of every  10 known infectious diseases in people  can be spread from animals  tetris adenom heads the world health  organization  wet markets are an important source of  affordable  food and livelihood for millions of  people all over the world but in many  places they have been  poorly regulated and poorly maintained  video obtained by cbs news from animal  rights activists  of live animal markets coast to coast  show unsanitary conditions  animals in cages stacked on top of each  other with feces  urine or blood able to flow between  cages  in terms of how sanitary these markets  are  did these markets pose any kind of  health  threat i wouldn't eat from one of those  markets  new york state legislator linda  rosenthal is alarmed at what she  says is the lack of oversight from state  and local governments  she has introduced a bill that would  shut down the markets  while the risk is evaluated it's really  a matter of  public health come see what we do  before you make past judgment we keep  all our animals separate  we sanitize multiple times a day it's  part of our routine before kovid  imramuden's family has run a live animal  market for 64 years  in queens new york and says they are  vital for customers wanting kosher or  halal meat for a lot of my clientele  it's a tradition  it's a way of preserving their way of  life since the covid crisis began new  york state has stopped inspecting live  animal markets  assemblywoman rosenthal has sponsored a  bill that would suspend the licenses of  existing live animal markets  and stop issuing any new ones for cbs  this morning saturday this is jim  axelrod  in montclair new jersey  there is much more news ahead
 

